Unique Value Proposition

For university students with limited income, the proposed budgeting app provides clear daily spending guidance and automatic spending control, helping them avoid overspending without complex budgeting processes.
Unlike traditional budgeting apps, this solution is: - Free or very low cost - Simple and student-focused - Designed to work offline - Focused on prevention, not just tracking.


User Personas for the Student Budgeting Application
Based on the qualitative data gathered from the student interviews, four distinct user personas have been developed. These personas represent the core segments of the student population who struggle with financial management and highlight the specific needs and pain points that the budgeting application must address.
1. Eddy: The Impulsive Spender Seeking a Barrier
Quote: "I don't think it would like make me change to buy something that I had my mind on. So if there is, like, a feature that limits me or makes it hard for me to make a purchase that is on a budget, that would be great."

Eddy is a typical student living off-campus who relies on a monthly allowance. His primary struggle is impulse control. He knows he should budget, but his mental tracking is easily overridden by the desire for immediate gratification, especially for food. He is not looking for a passive tracking tool; he is looking for an external barrier, a system that actively creates friction or a hard limit to prevent him from making purchases that will jeopardize his financial stability later in the month. His frustration stems from his own lack of self-discipline, and he needs the app to be the "bad cop" that he cannot be for himself.

