5. Manzi, a regular student who just wants to be a responsible spender

Manzi is a 2nd year male student at the University of Rwanda. He lives off campus, on a government-issued allowance, and has a side hustle. He is also able to use his phone for many of his endeavors, including budgeting. Manzi mainly spends his money on rent, food, transport, some school needs, and leisure. His primary goals include knowing exactly how much he can spend today without regret, avoiding reaching the end of the month with no money, and easy budgeting that doesn’t feel like accounting. Manzi currently budgets using writing lists and mental calculations; he needs an app to take care of the maths and force him to spend responsibly.


Competitor analysis (SWOT)
By competitor, we mean African budgeting apps. There are apps like FinanceAnt, Rehani, Jielewi, MobiKash,...

Strengths
The first strength many of them have is automatic transaction tracking via MPESA integration. It doesn’t require users to manually enter a lot of data and is able to access all the transactions the user makes as long as they’re digital. They also tailor budgeting based on the user’s income and goals, provide real-time insights,a nd are relevant to its local users, especially those who use mobile money.

Weaknesses
These apps’ weaknesses are that first: They are only limited to MPESA, second, unlike our solution they are not specific to students who have very limited financial resources.

Opportunities 
The main opportunity these apps have is a large and diverse target audience because many of them operate across East and Southern Africa. They also have the potential to integrate with banks and fintechs to strengthen their service provision.

Threats
One of the main threats they face is the many startups emerging in the region that do the same thing. Also, any digital platform that is integrated with financial institutions faces data privacy risks such as theft. Above all, there is a huge dependency on those mobile money systems.


 
 
