Design Reflection
When we looked at the interviews, the biggest issue was that most of us just "freestyle" our spending until we’re suddenly broke. We called this "Spendception"—where digital payments feel like fake money because you don't physically see it leaving your wallet.
To fix this, I made the "Safe to Spend" number the biggest thing on the dashboard. It gives you that instant reality check you miss when using a card. For students like Nkhomotabo who hate boring data entry, I kept the Add Expense screen super simple so it doesn't feel like a chore.
I also added "Panic Mode" and "Low Balance" alerts because our research showed that students actually avoid checking their apps when they’re stressed about money. Instead of making you go look for the bad news, the app tells you exactly when to stop spending. Finally, the "Savings Lock" was a direct request from Veronica to stop us from dipping into our rent money for a late-night pizza. It’s all about creating that "barrier" we found everyone was looking for.


